AN ADDRESS, A LOAD, AND A BRANCH DISAGREE ABOUT THE SAME BYTES.

Status: bottom-up English Major C description of a REAL,
temporary ARMv7-A T32 object-file byte sequence.
The bytes were assembled and read back.
The model's register and memory effects are a stated
architectural interpretation, NOT observed physical
processor execution or a working English Major C compiler.

THE EXACT MACHINE-BYTE ARTIFACT.

A short temporary ARM Thumb assembly source
contained precisely the following directives:

    .syntax unified
    .arch armv7-a
    .thumb
    .text
    .global address_read_branch
    .thumb_func
    address_read_branch:
        .hword 0xA000
        .hword 0x4901
        .hword 0xE002
        .hword 0xBE00
        .word 0x12345678
        .hword 0xBF00
        .hword 0xDF01

The foreign diagnostic compiler was
clang 17.0.0 with target armv7a-none-eabi
and Thumb state selected. It created
an ELF32 little-endian ARM object.
LLVM objdump 17.0.0 produced its
ordinary linear machine-code disassembly
and raw contents listing.

The raw .text bytes, in ascending
address order, were independently
confirmed to equal EXACTLY:

    00 A0 01 49 02 E0 00 BE
    78 56 34 12 00 BF 01 DF

There are sixteen bytes.
The instruction encodings and the
explicit data word are stored
in the same .text section.
The existence of the bytes establishes
neither a safe executable memory mapping
nor a program run on an actual ARM CPU.

SHA256 of transient diagnostic source:
    7bcf3e392d1dc62a9aece9425abfc198509509e9acaca4d6cdb2b3a93b71b3ad
SHA256 of transient ELF32 ARM object:
    e5627dbcebaffeed79ccb3851d0e6b7533b1e2d8f6fb8eb5b6a79d063895d10b
SHA256 of transient linear decoder output:
    c89da3af7a3c63df506f5958b89f4e169142de9b5422bcca2f6ad986415febd4
SHA256 of transient raw section dump:
    1f619f98b570557f15d4b4b1924b9728bfaa08d30842c2347a8eeb510e73225a

These are IDs of temporary diagnostic files,
not a promise that their toolchain can be
reconstructed bit-for-bit without its
full executable and dependency identity.
The object is not an English Major C build.

PART ONE. THE LINEAR DISASSEMBLER'S OUTPUT.

At relative byte offset 0x0000, LLVM displayed:
    a000  adr r0, #0

At offset 0x0002:
    4901  ldr r1, [pc, #4]

At offset 0x0004:
    e002  b 0x000C

At offset 0x0006:
    be00  bkpt #0

At offset 0x0008:
    5678  ldrsb r0, [r7, r1]

At offset 0x000A:
    1234  asrs r4, r6, #8

At offset 0x000C:
    bf00  nop

At offset 0x000E:
    df01  svc #1

The two lines at offsets 0x0008 and
0x000A are misleading FOR THIS PATH:
the original assembler explicitly placed
a single ordinary 32-bit literal DATA WORD
there, not those two named instructions.

The decoder can interpret the two
halfwords 0x5678 and 0x1234 as
native instruction encodings.
But their ability to be decoded is
NOT evidence that the execution index
ever selected their addresses.

The name and decoding output of a
disassembler are observational evidence
about byte interpretation, not a
complete causal instruction trace.

PART TWO. DECLARE A PRECISE MACHINE ENVIRONMENT.

Assume the .text section is loaded
at virtual BYTE ADDRESS 0x00001000,
aligned to a four-byte boundary.

Assume the complete sixteen-byte
region is executable as T32 code at
its declared actual instruction
entry points. Assume also that
the four-byte region at
0x00001008–0x0000100B
can be READ as ordinary little-endian
data, and contains the exact
bytes 78, 56, 34, 12.

The assumption of readable data
is separate from executable
permission. Do not generalize
that all executable memory
must also be readable on all
ARM operating systems.

Assume a single architectural
execution thread with no
intervening asynchronous interrupt,
no external mutation of bytes or
registers, and no unrelated fault
before the explicitly unsupported
instruction boundary.

The instruction index begins
at 0x00001000.
Every general-purpose register
including R0 and R1 is initially
UNKNOWN unless written below.
The four condition flags N/Z/C/V
are also initially UNKNOWN.
SP and LR are initially UNKNOWN.
No stack frame or valid return
address is assumed.

PART THREE. FIRST OPERATION: CALCULATE AN ADDRESS AS DATA.

At execution index 0x00001000,
the next encoded halfword is A000.
Its five high bits are 10100,
selecting the T1 ADR family.
The low register destination
field is zero, selecting R0.
The immediate byte is zero,
so the scaled BYTE offset is zero.

The instruction is the English
operation number 40, whose name
spells out computing an aligned
PC-relative address without
reading data memory.

The architectural PC operand
value is 0x1000+4=0x1004.
Round it down to four-byte
alignment; it remains 0x1004.

Add the offset zero. Store
the numerical value 0x1004
in R0. Preserve all other
general-purpose registers,
SP, LR, and the N/Z/C/V flags.

IMPORTANT: 0x1004 happens to
be the address of the actual
branch instruction located
two instructions later.
Merely storing that address
in R0 does not branch there.

The ADR itself is two bytes,
so the execution index advances
normally to 0x1002. It does
not take R0 as a new instruction
address.

After operation one:
    R0=0x00001004;
    memory unchanged;
    execution index=0x00001002.

PART FOUR. SECOND OPERATION: READ DATA AT A COMPUTED ADDRESS.

At execution index 0x00001002,
the next halfword is 4901.
Its fixed five high bits 01001
select literal T1 LDR.
The register field chooses R1.
The imm8 value is one, giving
a BYTE displacement of four.

The architectural PC operand
value is 0x1002+4=0x1006.
Align it down to a multiple
of four, giving 0x1004.
Add the scaled offset four,
giving DATA ADDRESS 0x1008.

Now and ONLY now request the
four-byte data-memory read
from that address. The
assumed little-endian four
bytes are 78,56,34,12,
so the register receives
the bit pattern 0x12345678.

The instruction writes
R1=0x12345678, NOT R1=0x1008.
It preserves R0=0x1004,
does not branch, and does
not alter N/Z/C/V.

The instruction is two bytes.
The sequential execution index
becomes 0x00001004.

PART FIVE. THIRD OPERATION: USE A BRANCH TO SKIP BOTH A BREAKPOINT
AND THE SOURCE'S ORDINARY DATA WORD.

At execution index 0x00001004,
the halfword E002 is the
existing operation 42, a
T32 B T2 short immediate branch.

Its immediate field is the
eleven-bit unsigned encoding 2,
interpreted as signed integer
positive two and then scaled
by two bytes. Its byte
displacement is +4.

Its architectural PC base
is 0x1004+4=0x1008.
The chosen branch target is
0x1008+4=0x100C.

This is an ordinary taken
branch outside IT, not a
flag-dependent branch or
a function call. N/Z/C/V
are not consulted or changed.
LR remains unknown and
unchanged by this B operation.

Consequently the execution
index becomes 0x0000100C.
The halfword at 0x1006,
BKPT encoding BE00, is
NOT architecturally executed.
The four bytes of literal
DATA at 0x1008–0x100B
are NOT architecturally
executed as instructions.

This distinction is about
architectural control flow.
A physical instruction-fetch
unit might speculatively
fetch bytes it later discards,
but doing so is NOT proof
that those bytes caused
register or debug effects.

PART SIX. FOURTH OPERATION: THE PERMITTED NO-OP.

At instruction address 0x100C,
the halfword BF00 is the
exact permitted native NOP
under operation number 52.

It does not change R0, R1,
any other register, flags
or data memory. It is
two bytes long, so the
execution index becomes
0x0000100E.

PART SEVEN. STOP AT THE FIRST UNGRANTED ARCHITECTURAL OPERATION.

At 0x100E, the halfword
DF01 is architecturally
SVC T1 with immediate
number one.

The native ARM decoder
understands it as a
supervisor-call exception
request. English Major C's
present permission set
does not grant SVC because
the process-specific
OS exception/ABI contract
has not been declared.

Stop translation at
that address. Do not
claim the operating system
performed a service,
returned a result, or
terminated a process.

At the blocked boundary,
the known architectural
state has:
    execution index 0x100E;
    R0 = 0x00001004;
    R1 = 0x12345678;
    SP = UNKNOWN;
    LR = UNKNOWN;
    N/Z/C/V = their unknown
              unchanged initial values;
    ordinary data memory
       unchanged by these
       successfully modeled instructions.

The ordered instruction-entry addresses
visited in this trace are:
    0x1000, 0x1002, 0x1004,
    0x100C, 0x100E (blocked).

None of the instruction-looking
bytes at 0x1008 or 0x100A
was executed in that trace.

PART EIGHT. SAME DATA BYTES, DIFFERENT ARCHITECTURAL ROLE.

The literal word's bit pattern
is 0x12345678. It could
be regarded as an unsigned
number, a signed number,
an address, a packed
representation, or
two 16-bit halfword
instruction encodings.

Those interpretations
do NOT cause native execution
on their own.

One actual LDR reads the
four bytes as one DATA WORD.
The native execution index
never points to either
of the literal's halfword
addresses in the traced path.
The linear disassembler's
LDRSB/ASRS display is
therefore a DECODE POSSIBILITY,
not a successful execution
trace for the current run.

Likewise ADR computes
the address of another
instruction and stores it
in R0, but execution
continues sequentially
instead of jumping to R0.

This demonstrates three
non-interchangeable operations
on closely related addresses:
  compute a numerical address;
  read data from a numerical address;
  transfer execution to another
  actual instruction address.

PART NINE. ALTERNATIVES AND THEIR REASONS.

Choice A: interleave literal
data and instruction bytes,
using an explicit branch
to skip the data.
This is the observed object
layout. It is valid under
the stated execution entry
and readable memory assumptions
but can mislead a linear
disassembler if its
code/data distinctions
are ignored.

Choice B: store the literal
in a separate data section.
Benefits: clearer data provenance
and possibly distinct permissions.
Costs: different linkage and
address reach. The raw machine
code would differ, so it cannot
be claimed as the observed one.

Choice C: compute a pointer
with ADR, and then load
through that pointer.
This may reproduce a
numerical result, but it
requires a separate actual
load with its own memory
effects. It is not the
one-instruction translation
of 4901.

Choice D: build the integer
constant directly through
MOVW/MOVT instead of using
literal memory. Avoids a
memory data read but changes
the native instruction
sequence and observable
fault/caching behavior.

Choice E: execute the literal
bytes as instructions by
branching to 0x1008.
This is a DIFFERENT PROGRAM.
It would require verifying
that 0x1008 is an authorized
instruction entry and that
both halfword decodings and
their effects are permitted.
It is not the program whose
branch E002 goes to 0x100C.

Choice F: rely on linear
disassembler output alone.
Rejected because code/data
classification and
reachability are missing.
The actual literal is
clearly data in the
assembler source, while a
decoder sees possible
instructions at the same
offsets.

Choice G: interpret the
same bytes through an
English Major C runtime.
This requires a real
parser/interpreter,
a chosen executable grant,
and explicit instruction
fetch versus data read.
It is a possible later
implementation strategy,
not one already executed.

Choice H: abstract the
actual machine into a
higher-level function
which returns 0x12345678.
This discards the execution-index
path, the ADR address update,
the branch, and the
ungranted SVC boundary.
That may be a later
justified abstraction
but it is not the desired
first bottom-up translation.

PART TEN. REQUIRED FUTURE INDEPENDENT ACCEPTANCE.

A checker must reject:
  1. giving R0=0x1002
     after ADR A000;
  2. treating ADR as a
     branch to R0=0x1004;
  3. giving R1=0x1008
     after LDR 4901;
  4. using A+4 unaligned
     as the LDR base at A=0x1002;
  5. moving the execution index
     from branch E002 to 0x1008;
  6. executing BE00 despite
     its skipped instruction address;
  7. executing the two
     literal halfwords 5678/1234
     as LDRSB/ASRS on this path;
  8. assuming all executable
     memory is readable on
     every possible target;
  9. asserting the data read
     never faults without the
     actual mapping contract;
 10. changing comparison
     flags without a
     flag-setting operation;
 11. pretending the
     ungranted SVC was
     executed or returned;
 12. treating the linear
     disassembly of the
     literal word as proof
     of architectural
     machine instruction execution;
 13. announcing that this
     diagnostic is an
     accepted English Major C
     compiler or device run.

The diagnostic assembled and disassembled
real bytes, and the expected register/index
state was checked by simple arithmetic.
No real ARM core, operating system exception,
or independently accepted English Major C
executable was run.

END OF THE CODE-AND-DATA AMBIGUITY SPECIMEN.
